   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Consider the curve (x^2 - 6x + 8)(1 - y^2) = 9, for which dy/dx = (x - 3)(1 - y^2)/(y(x^2 - 6x + 8)). For a positive constant k, the vertical line x = k is tangent to the curve. Find k."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Add",
         3,
         [
            "Sqrt",
            10
         ]
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "A vertical tangent needs the denominator of dy/dx to be zero with the numerator nonzero. The denominator is zero when y = 0, x = 2, or x = 4.",
         "mathjson": 0
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "At x = 2 or x = 4 the factor x^2 - 6x + 8 is 0, so the left side of the curve equation is 0, not 9. Neither x-value belongs to a point of the curve.",
         "mathjson": 0
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Substitute y = 0: x^2 - 6x + 8 = 9, so x^2 - 6x - 1 = 0, which gives x = 3 + sqrt(10) or x = 3 - sqrt(10).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            3,
            [
               "Sqrt",
               10
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "At both points the numerator is (x - 3)(1) = sqrt(10) or -sqrt(10), which is nonzero, so both tangents are vertical. Since 3 - sqrt(10) is negative, the positive value is k = 3 + sqrt(10).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            3,
            [
               "Sqrt",
               10
            ]
         ]
      }
   ],
